WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.146
Welcome to Lecture 10 of the

2
00:00:02.212 --> 00:00:03.531
FrontierCS 2 course

3
00:00:03.531 --> 00:00:05.180
on software engineering.

4
00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.038
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

5
00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:09.850
removes its key idea from

6
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.453
the authors' own code

7
00:00:11.453 --> 00:00:14.170
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

8
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.024
This field has one task: CREST

9
00:00:17.024 --> 00:00:19.043
a concolic tester for C

10
00:00:19.043 --> 00:00:20.739
from Burnim and Sen

11
00:00:20.739 --> 00:00:22.920
at ASE two thousand eight.

12
00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.549
The task removes its search strategy

13
00:00:25.611 --> 00:00:27.150
the rule that picks which

14
00:00:27.212 --> 00:00:28.629
condition to flip next

15
00:00:28.629 --> 00:00:30.230
and the agent writes one.

16
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.600
With one task, we go deeper

17
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.197
concolic testing on a tiny program

18
00:00:35.197 --> 00:00:37.289
a race of search strategies

19
00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:38.660
and then the task.

20
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.680
Concolic means concrete plus symbolic.

21
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.649
The tester runs the program on real inputs

22
00:00:45.709 --> 00:00:47.780
and at the same time records every

23
00:00:47.841 --> 00:00:50.155
condition along the path that depended

24
00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.190
on those inputs.

25
00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.205
Joined together, those conditions are the

26
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.700
run's path constraint.

27
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:57.690
Any input that satisfies

28
00:00:57.758 --> 00:00:59.330
it takes the same path.

29
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:00.955
To reach new code

30
00:01:00.955 --> 00:01:03.009
keep the first k conditions

31
00:01:03.009 --> 00:01:04.426
negate condition k

32
00:01:04.426 --> 00:01:06.480
and ask a solver for inputs.

33
00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.497
The next run follows the old

34
00:01:08.557 --> 00:01:10.104
path up to that condition

35
00:01:10.104 --> 00:01:11.950
and then takes the other side.

36
00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.218
Here is a toy of our own

37
00:01:14.218 --> 00:01:17.188
a packet parser over twelve small integers

38
00:01:17.188 --> 00:01:19.010
each from zero to fifteen.

39
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.472
The header has ten conditions

40
00:01:21.472 --> 00:01:22.880
arranged as a tree.

41
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.750
Then a loop checks each

42
00:01:24.816 --> 00:01:26.270
payload byte for zero.

43
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.878
Every condition has two branches: taken

44
00:01:29.878 --> 00:01:31.100
and not taken.

45
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.320
So eleven conditions give

46
00:01:33.394 --> 00:01:34.890
twenty-two branches.

47
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.010
The loop's condition appears

48
00:01:37.073 --> 00:01:38.590
six times on every path

49
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:40.360
with the same two branches.

50
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.300
Take one input.

51
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.042
Its path passes three checks

52
00:01:45.042 --> 00:01:48.671
x zero is below eight, x zero equals three

53
00:01:48.671 --> 00:01:50.650
and x one equals seven.

54
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.399
Then it fails the fourth check

55
00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:55.720
x two equals x one plus four.

56
00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.070
Now negate that condition.

57
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.536
Our solver may change only the

58
00:02:00.606 --> 00:02:02.581
inputs this condition reads

59
00:02:02.581 --> 00:02:03.780
x one and x two.

60
00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.288
But the earlier check, x one equals seven

61
00:02:07.288 --> 00:02:10.370
also reads x one, so it must still hold.

62
00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.236
The solver tries values in order

63
00:02:13.236 --> 00:02:15.600
and x two equals eleven works.

64
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.179
The new run follows the old path

65
00:02:18.179 --> 00:02:19.666
takes the other side

66
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:21.490
and reaches a condition no

67
00:02:21.557 --> 00:02:22.976
earlier run had seen

68
00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.070
x three greater than thirteen.

69
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.069
Pause and predict.

70
00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:29.275
A run passed x three equals

71
00:02:29.354 --> 00:02:32.821
two times x four, and x five equals fifteen

72
00:02:32.821 --> 00:02:36.210
and then failed x four greater than seven.

73
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.640
Negate it: what happens?

74
00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.850
The negation is infeasible.

75
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.280
x four above seven would need x

76
00:02:46.347 --> 00:02:48.293
three to be at least sixteen

77
00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:50.440
but the inputs stop at fifteen.

78
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.027
So the solver returns nothing

79
00:02:53.027 --> 00:02:55.190
one solver call, and no run.

80
00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:58.430
Why not just try random inputs?

81
00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.864
A random input passes x one equals seven

82
00:03:01.941 --> 00:03:04.408
only if x zero is three as well

83
00:03:04.408 --> 00:03:07.260
one chance in two hundred fifty-six.

84
00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:09.733
Each further equality divides

85
00:03:09.806 --> 00:03:11.050
by sixteen again.

86
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.868
Of one hundred thousand random inputs

87
00:03:13.933 --> 00:03:15.426
only two passed x three

88
00:03:15.491 --> 00:03:16.920
greater than thirteen.

89
00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.101
Concolic testing starts from all zeros

90
00:03:20.101 --> 00:03:21.190
as CREST does.

91
00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.742
It flips one failed check per run

92
00:03:23.742 --> 00:03:25.620
and gets there in five runs.

93
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.731
So: a path constraint is the list of

94
00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:30.840
conditions a run depended on.

95
00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:34.373
Keep a prefix, negate one condition, solve

96
00:03:34.373 --> 00:03:36.150
and you get a new path.

97
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:37.875
And equality checks

98
00:03:37.875 --> 00:03:39.799
which defeat random inputs

99
00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:41.380
are easy for a solver.

100
00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:42.674
As an exercise

101
00:03:42.674 --> 00:03:45.444
always flip the last condition of the path

102
00:03:45.507 --> 00:03:47.459
and see whether it ever reaches

103
00:03:47.522 --> 00:03:49.410
x three greater than thirteen.

104
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.160
Next: which condition to flip.
